"<p><strong>Prepared for:</strong> Tillmans Tranquils<br><strong>Review date:</strong> 3 November 2026<br><strong>Website:</strong> <a href=\"https://www.tillmanstranquils.com/\">https://www.tillmanstranquils.com/</a></p>\n<p>I reviewed your website against the 15 points in your correction document. Several requested changes are already visible on the pages I checked. My plan is to complete the remaining work, verify the existing changes across the relevant templates, and provide a completion record for each point.</p>\n<h2 id=\"summary\">Summary</h2>\n<h3>1. Hours per point and the total</h3>\n<p>My estimated allowance is <strong>28 hours</strong>. This includes <strong>27 hours 40 minutes for the task work</strong> and <strong>20 minutes to duplicate the theme and check its preview, if needed</strong>. I have allowed minutes for small fixes and reused the existing implementations when estimating larger tasks, with no minimum charge per point.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Point</th>\n<th>Work I will complete or verify</th>\n<th align=\"right\">Estimated time</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Add the two tag-path redirects and check the source links/generator</td>\n<td align=\"right\">20 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Remove the conflicting product noindex rule; check the listed products and existing redirect</td>\n<td align=\"right\">45 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Add the requested footer/contextual links and reconcile the 23 original targets</td>\n<td align=\"right\">1 hour 30 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Verify the two corrected homepage links</td>\n<td align=\"right\">Included in point 1; no additional time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Process the 57 listed image sources, handle heavy animations and update their references</td>\n<td align=\"right\">6 hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Complete alt-text rules, unique-image descriptions and the agreed future-image handling</td>\n<td align=\"right\">4 hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Find/correct the double-slash source link and test the requested redirect method</td>\n<td align=\"right\">20 minutes; redirect method to be confirmed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Verify the existing Organization/US correction across collection templates</td>\n<td align=\"right\">15 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Check the existing dynamic FAQ output, matching answers and no-FAQ behavior</td>\n<td align=\"right\">30 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Reconcile product/review schema sources, review mappings and conflicting counts</td>\n<td align=\"right\">4 hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>Map the collection aliases and test supported redirect behavior</td>\n<td align=\"right\">30 minutes; redirect method to be confirmed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>Adjust the existing review block to the reference design and reconcile its collection data</td>\n<td align=\"right\">3 hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>13</td>\n<td>Address the measured performance bottlenecks and repeat the checks</td>\n<td align=\"right\">5 hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>14</td>\n<td>Verify removal of the old block across collection templates</td>\n<td align=\"right\">Included in point 12; no additional time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>15</td>\n<td>Verify and correct the existing sidebar across templates and actual mobile widths</td>\n<td align=\"right\">1 hour 30 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Task subtotal</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Overlapping points counted once</strong></td>\n<td align=\"right\"><strong>27 hours 40 minutes</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Testing setup, if needed</td>\n<td>Duplicate the theme and check its preview</td>\n<td align=\"right\">20 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Total allowance</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Task work and duplicate-theme setup, if needed</strong></td>\n<td align=\"right\"><strong>28 hours</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>For example, I have allowed approximately 10 minutes for a simple broken-link edit or redirect entry. I have not added a separate charge for the homepage links that are already corrected. The longer tasks involve batch media processing, template changes or review-data reconciliation.</p>\n<p>My estimate assumes I can retain the current review integration and shared sections, process the images in batches, and use the final references you provide. For points 7 and 11, I need to confirm a supported method for the requested redirects before I can commit to their complete implementation. The listed time covers the source checks, URL mapping and feasibility checks; it does not include creating new redirect infrastructure.</p>\n<h3>2. My hourly rate</h3>\n<p><strong>My hourly rate is US$15.</strong> At the estimated allowance of 28 hours, the labour cost would be <strong>US$420</strong>. If duplicate-theme setup is not needed, that time will not be billed.</p>\n<p>If any of the uncertainties outlined in this report require work that could exceed this allowance, I will discuss it with you before proceeding.</p>\n<p>If a paid app or another third-party cost is necessary, I will discuss it with you before incurring it.</p>\n<h3>3. Realistic delivery date for the full list</h3>\n<p><strong>I propose delivery by Monday 9 November 2026 at 6:00 p.m. WAT</strong>, starting Wednesday 4 November, provided I receive the required access, references and decisions before starting. This allows <strong>four working days</strong>, at approximately seven working hours per day, with the weekend between them.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Wednesday 4 November:</strong> confirm the testing setup, duplicate the theme if needed, settle the redirect-method questions, complete the short URL/indexing/internal-link tasks and begin schema corrections.</li>\n<li><strong>Thursday 5 November:</strong> finish product/review schema work, the collection reviews block and sidebar checks.</li>\n<li><strong>Friday 6 November:</strong> complete image processing and alt-text work.</li>\n<li><strong>Monday 9 November:</strong> finish performance work, complete the final checks and send the completion record.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>I will confirm this schedule with you once access and the outstanding decisions are in place. Before committing to the full-list deadline, I need to resolve the redirect limitations in points 7 and 11, the reference review-schema requirement in point 12, and the performance acceptance target in point 13. I will identify any unresolved point explicitly rather than mark it complete. If access or answers arrive later, I will update the delivery date with you. Google&#39;s subsequent crawling and indexing updates are outside the implementation timeline.</p>\n<h3>4. What I need from you to start</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Shopify collaborator access</strong> to the relevant theme/code, menus, content, products, collections and redirects, plus access to your staging/development store if one is available.</li>\n<li><strong>Access to your Yotpo/review integration and relevant SEO app settings</strong>, where those apps control reviews, robots tags or structured data.</li>\n<li><strong>The Ahrefs export referenced in your document</strong>, including affected URLs and link-source information.</li>\n<li><strong>The final review-block reference screenshot</strong> and the relevant section/schema code or access for the hipuffy/nothingbutcanna examples.</li>\n<li><strong>Access to the listed source media</strong>, including editable animation files where available, and authorization to upload the optimized replacements/video alternatives requested in point 5.</li>\n<li><strong>Your answers to the questions below</strong>, a main contact for clarification, and agreement on the testing approach, start date, delivery deadline and hourly rate.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If you do not have a staging store, I can use an unpublished duplicate theme. I have included 20 minutes for duplicate-theme setup and a preview check if needed. Setting up a separate staging store is not included. Changes to menus, redirects, products or app settings may affect shared store data, so I will agree the testing approach with you before making those changes.</p>\n<h3>5. Questions, numbered by task point</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Task point</th>\n<th>My question or requested input</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>All points — testing setup</strong></td>\n<td>Do you have a staging or development store for testing, or should I use an unpublished duplicate theme? For changes that affect shared store data or settings, such as menus, redirects, products or apps, how would you prefer testing to be handled?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>2</strong></td>\n<td>Please send the Ahrefs export so I can check the complete affected-product list.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>3</strong></td>\n<td>Which destination should I use for the missing reviews page? Where would you like the two program links within the footer/business navigation specified in your document?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>5</strong></td>\n<td>Do you have editable/original files for the heavy animations, in case I cannot convert the downloaded versions cleanly? I will retain the under-200-KB target or video alternative you requested.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>6</strong></td>\n<td>Do you need future-image autofill saved to Shopify&#39;s media metadata when an image is uploaded, or is automatic storefront alt text from product/title data sufficient?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>7</strong></td>\n<td>Is there already a supported server/edge redirect mechanism for the live double-slash URL? I need to confirm how the requested 301 can be delivered.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>10</strong></td>\n<td>Please provide review-integration access. Can you confirm that aggregate totals should cover all genuine published reviews while the individual embedded reviews may be limited to the most recent 50, as in your example?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>11</strong></td>\n<td>Do you have an existing redirect solution for live .html aliases? Shopify restricts these native redirects, so I need to establish a supported method with you before committing to that outcome.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>12</strong></td>\n<td>Please send the final screenshot and referenced implementation. If its collection review schema conflicts with current Google eligibility requirements, can we agree the schema treatment before I implement it?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>13</strong></td>\n<td>Which result should define completion: agreed LCP/blocking-time improvements, a PageSpeed score, or resolution of specific diagnostics? Your document does not specify a numeric target.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>15</strong></td>\n<td>May I test against the storefront&#39;s existing mobile breakpoints, or are there particular device widths you need included? I will keep the description above the products on mobile as requested.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>I have no further questions on points 1, 4, 8, 9 or 14 at this stage; I will follow the instructions in your document.</p>\n<nav aria-label=\"Report contents\"><strong>Go to a task</strong><div class=\"links\"><a href=\"#issue-1\">1. 404 page URLs</a>\n<a href=\"#issue-2\">2. Noindex product pages and legacy product URLs</a>\n<a href=\"#issue-3\">3. Orphan pages and requested internal links</a>\n<a href=\"#issue-4\">4. Homepage links to broken pages</a>\n<a href=\"#issue-5\">5. Image file sizes</a>\n<a href=\"#issue-6\">6. Missing alt attributes and future-image handling</a>\n<a href=\"#issue-7\">7. Double slash in the article URL</a>\n<a href=\"#issue-8\">8. Organization markup on collection pages</a>\n<a href=\"#issue-9\">9. FAQ markup</a>\n<a href=\"#issue-10\">10. Reviews in product schema</a>\n<a href=\"#issue-11\">11. 301 redirects for html collection URLs</a>\n<a href=\"#issue-12\">12. New reviews block on collection pages</a>\n<a href=\"#issue-13\">13. Website performance recommendations</a>\n<a href=\"#issue-14\">14. Remove the old collection reviews block</a>\n<a href=\"#issue-15\">15. Category description in a left sidebar</a></div></nav><h2>How I reviewed the site</h2>\n<p>I checked the site against your document&#39;s required actions and developer instructions. I inspected page source, HTTP responses and redirect chains, image responses, robots/canonical tags, structured data, sitemaps and browser output, and generated mobile/desktop PageSpeed results. My sample included eight collections, the flagged product and additional products, all 23 original orphan-page candidates, the reported tag variants and the five .html examples.</p>\n<p>The findings below are from my 3 November review. I have not treated sampled results as proof that every template is complete. Where a change is already visible, I will verify its coverage rather than rebuild it. I still need the Ahrefs export and reference-project implementation to confirm the full scope they describe.</p>\n<p>For each point, I have set out your requested changes, what I found, what I will do, how I will verify it and the estimated time. I have retained the original numbering so you can compare this proposal with your correction document.</p>\n<h2>How I have estimated the work</h2>\n<p>The task estimates below total 27 hours 40 minutes. The summary adds 20 minutes for duplicate-theme setup if needed, bringing the full allowance to 28 hours. Points 4 and 14 are included in their paired tasks, and point 13 excludes the image-processing time under point 5. I have identified unresolved platform or integration requirements under the relevant points; if they could take the work beyond the allowance, I will discuss that with you before proceeding.</p>\n<h2 id=\"issue-1\">1. 404 page URLs</h2>\n<p><strong>Your requested changes.</strong> Investigate <code>/tagged/cbd-info</code> and <code>/tagged/health</code>; find and correct every internal link to them; restore the intended destinations or set up relevant 301 redirects; check automatic tag-link generation; verify that internal links no longer lead to the missing pages.</p>\n<p><strong>What I found.</strong> Both short paths still return 404. The homepage now links to <code>/blogs/blog/tagged/cbd-info</code> and <code>/blogs/blog/tagged/health</code>, and those full archive paths return 200. The homepage-link part of the request is implemented in the fetched source. The two old destinations have not been shown to redirect.</p>\n<p><strong>What I will do.</strong> I will:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Search the active theme, menus, homepage sections and stored page/article content for both short paths.</li>\n<li>Correct any remaining references to the working intended destinations.</li>\n<li>Use the working blog-tag destinations when mapping the old paths, following your document&#39;s restore-or-redirect requirement.</li>\n<li>Inspect the component generating tag links so newly rendered links include the full blog path.</li>\n<li>Recrawl the changed links and affected templates after deployment.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Why this work is needed.</strong> Correct hrefs address the source of the problem. The requested destination handling catches old references, and fixing the generator prevents recurrence.</p>\n<p><strong>How I will verify completion.</strong> Neither short path appears as an internal destination; the intended pages return 200; any selected 301 is confirmed from the HTTP response; the generating component outputs valid paths; the follow-up broken-link result is retained.</p>\n<p><strong>Estimate: 20 minutes, including point 4.</strong> Approximately 10 minutes for the two known redirect entries and 10 minutes for the existing source/generator check and response verification, assuming the expected shared component. Other broken URLs are outside this point.</p>\n<h2 id=\"issue-2\">2. Noindex product pages and legacy product URLs</h2>\n<p><strong>Your requested changes.</strong> Remove noindex/nofollow from product URLs listed in the export, redirect old <code>/product/</code> paths to <code>/products/</code>, and leave the blog tag archives noindexed.</p>\n<p><strong>What I found.</strong> The Lemon Lime product, with and without the trailing slash, contains both <code>noindex,nofollow</code> and a separate <code>index, follow</code> robots tag. The restrictive directive remains a failure against the requested product-indexing outcome. The old <code>/product/lemon-lime-delta-8-thc-candy-drops/</code> address already returns a 301 to the Shopify product path. Checked blog tag variants contain <code>noindex, follow</code>, consistent with your document&#39;s instruction to preserve their exclusion.</p>\n<p><strong>What I will do.</strong> I will:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Identify the theme condition, app setting or product setting that emits the Lemon Lime noindex/nofollow tag and remove that restriction for the specified product.</li>\n<li>Reconcile the complete original export with the product URLs shown in the document, then check every applicable product URL rather than closing the task from one example.</li>\n<li>Verify the legacy-to-current product mappings and retain the working Lemon Lime redirect.</li>\n<li>Confirm that the shared change leaves <code>/blogs/blog/tagged/</code> exclusions unchanged.</li>\n<li>Check both HTML and applicable response headers for remaining indexing restrictions.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Why this work is needed.</strong> This implements your document&#39;s distinction between indexable product pages and intentionally excluded tag archives. Adding an index tag while leaving noindex in place does not resolve the requested correction.</p>\n<p><strong>How I will verify completion.</strong> Listed product destinations return 200 without the unwanted directives; legacy product addresses permanently redirect to the matching current product; tag archives retain their intended noindex output. I will also recheck the rate-limited product if it is part of your affected-URL list.</p>\n<p><strong>Estimate: 45 minutes.</strong> Assumes one shared robots condition or a small set of settings, then checks against the supplied affected-product list. If I find a different cause, I will let you know before changing the estimate.</p>\n<h2 id=\"issue-3\">3. Orphan pages and requested internal links</h2>\n<p><strong>Your requested changes.</strong> Add internal links to the listed pages from relevant locations; put shipping/refunds/tracking in footer navigation; give program pages suitable business/partner links; link a retained reviews page from products or the footer; keep one shipping-policy page and redirect the duplicate.</p>\n<p><strong>What I found.</strong> Shipping Policy is linked from the footer. <code>/pages/shipping-policy-1</code> already permanently redirects to <code>/pages/shipping-policy</code>. Sixteen other listed pages have links from the public HTML sitemap. Those links improve reachability but do not establish the specific footer and contextual placements requested in the document. <code>/pages/review-page</code> returns 404. Four original candidates had no incoming links from other pages in the checked sample; a complete crawl is needed to establish their full inlink status.</p>\n<p><strong>What I will do.</strong> I will:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Check all 23 original targets against their current working destinations.</li>\n<li>Add the specified footer/support links for retained shipping, refund and order-tracking pages.</li>\n<li>Add the program links in a relevant partner/business or about-page context.</li>\n<li>Resolve the missing reviews destination before adding its requested link; link the working destination from the specified relevant location.</li>\n<li>Add appropriate links to the other retained targets and record any target whose intended destination or purpose requires clarification.</li>\n<li>Preserve and verify the shipping-policy redirect already implemented.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Why this work is needed.</strong> The requested navigation placements make the pages accessible in the intended browsing context. A sitemap entry alone should not be used to mark those more specific instructions complete.</p>\n<p><strong>How I will verify completion.</strong> Every original target has a recorded result; requested navigation placements exist; links lead to working destinations; the shipping duplicate redirects; a final crawl confirms incoming links for the retained pages. Existing sitemap links do not require rebuilding.</p>\n<p><strong>Estimate: 1 hour 30 minutes.</strong> Covers the 23-target reconciliation and the requested navigation/contextual links, assuming you provide the intended working destinations and no new page content needs writing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"issue-4\">4. Homepage links to broken pages</h2>\n<p><strong>Your requested changes.</strong> Replace the two incorrect homepage tag links with the full working <code>/blogs/blog/tagged/</code> paths, or remove the links if no equivalent exists.</p>\n<p><strong>What I found.</strong> The fetched homepage contains the correct full paths. Both intended tag destinations load successfully. The specific source-link defect reported in the document was not reproduced.</p>\n<p><strong>What I will do.</strong> I will verify the active desktop and mobile homepage sections/navigation, ensure neither old href survives in another shared block, and include these links in issue 1&#39;s post-deployment check.</p>\n<p><strong>Why this work is needed.</strong> This verifies the exact repair already observed, while avoiding duplicate implementation work for the same two links.</p>\n<p><strong>How I will verify completion.</strong> Both homepage links lead directly to the intended working archives, and the obsolete paths are absent from the active homepage components.</p>\n<p><strong>Estimate: 0 additional minutes.</strong> The already-corrected homepage hrefs are verified within point 1; there is no separate charge.</p>\n<h2 id=\"issue-5\">5. Image file sizes</h2>\n<p><strong>Your requested changes.</strong> Reduce the listed oversized image downloads; use appropriate widths and srcset; compress images where needed; reduce the approximately 5 MB animation files to the specified under-200-KB target or use video for animation.</p>\n<p><strong>What I found.</strong> Your document contains 98 image URL entries representing 57 unique source paths. I retested representative assets during my review. Shopify returned WebP for those requests, including a historical animation at about 2.98 MB and another at about 2.31 MB. Sampled still images were much smaller, including approximately 243 KB and 210 KB. These observations do not establish that all 57 sources meet your document&#39;s requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>What I will do.</strong> I will:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Build a checklist for your document&#39;s complete image list, grouping multiple width URLs for the same source.</li>\n<li>Locate where each listed source is used and identify the served sizes on desktop and mobile.</li>\n<li>Correct img/srcset width selection where it is sending more data than required.</li>\n<li>Compress or replace the listed still-image sources that remain above the agreed document target.</li>\n<li>Convert the heavy animations to suitable video where that is the selected method permitted by the document, then update the referring sections.</li>\n<li>Retest all listed assets or their replacements, record final sizes/formats and visually check image clarity.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Why this work is needed.</strong> Width selection and compression address actual download size, while a video replacement can preserve animation without the original GIF transfer cost. Grouping URL variants prevents processing the same source repeatedly.</p>\n<p><strong>How I will verify completion.</strong> Every listed source has a recorded outcome; replacements display in their intended sections; images remain clear; served files meet the stated target or your document&#39;s video alternative. I will identify listed sources that are no longer used and distinguish them from files I have optimized. I will agree any necessary size exception with you.</p>\n<p><strong>Estimate: 6 hours.</strong> Assumes batch processing of the 57 unique listed sources, reuse of current template references and a small number of shared animation conversions. If editable media is unavailable or substantial manual rework is needed, I will discuss the time impact with you first.</p>\n<h2 id=\"issue-6\">6. Missing alt attributes and future-image handling</h2>\n<p><strong>Your requested changes.</strong> Cover products/cards, collections, homepage, landing pages and blogs; populate product/collection alternatives from their data; write meaningful descriptions for unique banners; use article/caption context for blog media; provide future-image autofill; keep decorative images empty but with an alt attribute.</p>\n<p><strong>What I found.</strong> Meaningful product alternatives now exist, but coverage is incomplete. The fetched homepage contains 4 img elements without alt attributes, CBD Mints contains 1, and a sampled sleep product contains 17. These are rendered-source counts, not counts of unique images. Empty alternatives also occur, and the document expressly permits them for decorative content.</p>\n<p><strong>What I will do.</strong> I will:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Check each page type named in the document, including product cards and unique landing-page sections.</li>\n<li>Preserve meaningful media-specific text; provide the requested product/collection context when the existing text is absent.</li>\n<li>Write descriptions for informative homepage and landing-page graphics.</li>\n<li>Add suitable blog image alternatives from the caption or article context where that accurately describes the image.</li>\n<li>Give decorative media an empty alt attribute.</li>\n<li>Implement and verify future-image autofill from the relevant product/title data, preserving manually authored values.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Why this work is needed.</strong> Shared-template changes cover repeated images; manual descriptions address unique graphics; future-image behavior prevents the reported gap from returning with new content.</p>\n<p><strong>How I will verify completion.</strong> Each required page type passes an attribute and meaning check, decorative media stays empty, and a new-image test demonstrates the specified autofill behavior. I will show whether autofill is provided during storefront rendering or saved to the uploaded media, and verify the behavior you select.</p>\n<p><strong>Estimate: 4 hours.</strong> Covers shared template rules, a bounded pass over unique informative images and straightforward future-image handling. I need your answer on saved upload metadata before starting; this estimate does not include building a custom integration.</p>\n<h2 id=\"issue-7\">7. Double slash in the article URL</h2>\n<p><strong>Your requested changes.</strong> Find and correct the internal source link; add a 301 from the double-slash article path to the single-slash path. Your document describes general repeated-slash normalization as optional.</p>\n<p><strong>What I found.</strong> The malformed address still returns 200. The clean article also returns 200. A clean canonical is present, but it is not the requested HTTP redirect. The originating malformed href was not located in the checked page sample.</p>\n<p><strong>What I will do.</strong> I will search article content, menus and theme output for the malformed path; correct any reference found; determine whether the active platform can issue the specified redirect; implement it through a supported mechanism if feasible; verify the response and final destination.</p>\n<p><strong>Why this work is needed.</strong> Correcting the href stops generation of the malformed link. The requested permanent redirect handles visits to the old address.</p>\n<p><strong>Technical dependency.</strong> Shopify&#39;s native redirects generally do not override a valid live 200 page. Therefore, entering a redirect rule is not proof that the required 301 will execute. I will keep this point open until I establish a supported implementation; I will not present a canonical tag as completion of your requested 301. <a href=\"https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/online-store/menus-and-links/url-redirect\">Shopify redirect documentation</a></p>\n<p><strong>How I will verify completion.</strong> No internal href contains the reported double slash; the malformed address returns an actual 301 to the intended clean article; no redirect loop occurs. I will check general normalization separately if we include that optional instruction in the agreed scope.</p>\n<p><strong>Estimate: 20 minutes for the source-link check/correction and redirect-method test.</strong> The requested 301 remains dependent on a supported mechanism. General normalization is optional in the document and is not automatically included.</p>\n<h2 id=\"issue-8\">8. Organization markup on collection pages</h2>\n<p><strong>Your requested changes.</strong> Apply the specified Organization markup to collection pages and use <code>US</code> for addressCountry, with the business information provided in the document.</p>\n<p><strong>What I found.</strong> The inspected collections already contain the requested Organization/address block with <code>addressCountry: &quot;US&quot;</code>. This supports observed implementation of the specific type/country correction in the sample. It does not prove all collection template assignments use the same block.</p>\n<p><strong>What I will do.</strong> I will locate the shared block, compare the requested organization/address/contact fields with the document, verify its placement across the applicable collection templates, and correct missing instances or deviations in that requested block.</p>\n<p><strong>Why this work is needed.</strong> A shared implementation gives existing and future collections the requested business description without adding it manually to each page.</p>\n<p><strong>How I will verify completion.</strong> Applicable collection templates output the requested Organization block, with the specified two-letter country representation; the block parses as JSON; the intended organization fields are present. If any business/contact value needs confirmation, I will ask you before changing it.</p>\n<p><strong>Estimate: 15 minutes.</strong> The requested block is already present in the sample; this covers a shared-template/field check and limited correction, not a schema rebuild.</p>\n<h2 id=\"issue-9\">9. FAQ markup</h2>\n<p><strong>Your requested changes.</strong> Add FAQPage output for every collection with an FAQ section; generate each collection&#39;s questions and answers dynamically from its own visible content; omit it where no FAQ exists; keep schema content aligned with visible text.</p>\n<p><strong>What I found.</strong> FAQPage is present on all eight checked collections. CBD Mints contains 10 question/answer entries, CBD Gummies 3, Delta 9 Gummies 10 and THC Mints 11. The requested feature exists in the sample; dynamic source selection, exact text parity and all-template behavior remain unverified.</p>\n<p><strong>What I will do.</strong> I will inspect the shared FAQ section and its Liquid output, confirm both visible content and JSON-LD use the same collection-specific data, check every assigned FAQ configuration, and test a collection with no FAQ. I will correct missing or mismatched output within the existing implementation.</p>\n<p><strong>Why this work is needed.</strong> A shared source prevents one collection receiving another&#39;s answers and reduces the risk of the visible and structured versions diverging.</p>\n<p><strong>How I will verify completion.</strong> Requested questions/answers appear accurately in the output, special characters produce valid JSON, no-FAQ pages omit unrelated FAQ markup, and current/future collection templates inherit the behavior.</p>\n<p><strong>Validation limitation.</strong> Google&#39;s FAQ rich-result feature was retired, so your document&#39;s Rich Results Test detection requirement cannot be used as originally written. I will still verify the FAQ implementation you requested through source and JSON checks. The retired Google display feature is not an available delivery outcome. <a href=\"https://developers.google.com/search/updates\">Google documentation updates</a></p>\n<p><strong>Estimate: 30 minutes.</strong> Checks the existing shared implementation, sampled answer parity and no-FAQ behavior; if shared logic is missing, I will explain the additional work to you before extending the estimate.</p>\n<h2 id=\"issue-10\">10. Reviews in product schema</h2>\n<p><strong>Your requested changes.</strong> Remove conflicting duplicate product output and any invented self-review; use real Yotpo reviews with author, body, publication date and rating; keep aggregates consistent with real review data; use ProductGroup for variant products; apply the implementation through product templates.</p>\n<p><strong>What I found.</strong> Customer-named reviews are present. Five successful additional product samples each expose 50 reviews, all with reviewBody and datePublished. Competing product outputs remain, including different aggregate counts for the same product. The Lemon Lime page also exposes a separate product rating block for a different product. The specific brand-authored Review described in your original document was not reproduced in the checked output.</p>\n<p><strong>What I will do.</strong> I will:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Identify the theme/app sources producing the competing product and review blocks.</li>\n<li>Retain one coherent main product or ProductGroup implementation, preserving legitimate variant objects and offers.</li>\n<li>Verify product-to-Yotpo mappings and pull genuine published reviews for the correct product.</li>\n<li>Verify author, reviewBody, datePublished and rating fields; check the requested recent-review selection.</li>\n<li>Reconcile the summary values and counts with the real review dataset and visible widget.</li>\n<li>Apply the correction through each applicable product template and test zero-review and variant cases.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Why this work is needed.</strong> This directly addresses your document&#39;s concern that product schema and displayed reviews must describe the same genuine product experience, without conflicting copies.</p>\n<p><strong>How I will verify completion.</strong> No conflicting main-product output or invented review remains; required review fields are populated from real data; product identity, ratings and totals agree; valid ProductGroup variants and offers are retained. Several Product variant objects inside a group are not automatically the duplicate defect the document is asking to remove.</p>\n<p><strong>Count clarification.</strong> I will validate the full real review total separately from the number of individual reviews embedded in a single response. Your example permits up to 50 recent reviews, so a product with more total reviews need not embed all of them. This lets me verify consistent totals while retaining the recent-review subset in your example.</p>\n<p><strong>Estimate: 4 hours.</strong> Assumes the existing review connection and ProductGroup implementation can be retained while competing outputs and mappings are corrected. A new integration is not included.</p>\n<h2 id=\"issue-11\">11. 301 redirects for html collection URLs</h2>\n<p><strong>Your requested changes.</strong> Create permanent redirects for every applicable <code>.html</code> collection address to its matching current collection; the five examples are a sample, not the whole required list. Use explicit mappings where supported.</p>\n<p><strong>What I found.</strong> The CBD Mints, THC Mints and CBD Gummies <code>.html</code> addresses still return 200 without the requested redirects. The old Delta 9 and CBD <code>.html</code> examples return 404. Their current collections use renamed paths, so the correct destinations must be mapped rather than inferred solely by removing the extension.</p>\n<p><strong>What I will do.</strong> I will inventory current collections and known historical aliases; build the complete source/destination list; account for renamed collections; determine whether the specified redirects can execute on the current Shopify setup; implement supported mappings; test each response and destination. I will list any unavailable mapping or platform-limited case as an unresolved item.</p>\n<p><strong>Why this work is needed.</strong> The complete map covers your document&#39;s all-collection requirement and prevents redirects to outdated or missing collection paths.</p>\n<p><strong>Technical dependency.</strong> Shopify explicitly states that native redirects cannot redirect a <code>.html</code> URL to the same URL without <code>.html</code>, and they do not override valid live pages. A bulk import does not remove that limitation. I will retain the requested 301 as the completion requirement and will not treat a canonical tag as a substitute. <a href=\"https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/online-store/menus-and-links/url-redirect\">Shopify redirect documentation</a></p>\n<p><strong>How I will verify completion.</strong> Every required source has a confirmed permanent redirect to the intended current collection, or a recorded unresolved dependency. A final destination returning 200 is not sufficient without recording the initial redirect response.</p>\n<p><strong>Estimate: 30 minutes for collection-alias mapping and redirect feasibility/response checks.</strong> Executing every requested 301 depends on a supported method. This is not a complete execution quote for an unknown redirect layer.</p>\n<h2 id=\"issue-12\">12. New reviews block on collection pages</h2>\n<p><strong>Your requested changes.</strong> Match your supplied reference, render through collection templates on existing and future collections, use real reviews for products in each collection, keep rating totals consistent with the data, include the specified structured data without conflicting duplication, and omit review schema where no real reviews exist.</p>\n<p><strong>What I found.</strong> All eight checked collections contain the new review section. Customer cards, author names, verification labels and product links are present. This establishes feature presence, not a complete visual match. The Delta 8 section reports one review while embedding 16 entries; that data discrepancy relates directly to the requested aggregate consistency.</p>\n<p><strong>Design details I will check and correct.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Requested customer-review heading and aggregate rating/stars/count presentation.</li>\n<li>Three cards per desktop row, with left/right carousel controls.</li>\n<li>Per-card rating, review text, customer name and genuine Verified badge.</li>\n<li>Product thumbnail and product name/link at the bottom of each card.</li>\n<li>Monospace text inside cards and the vertical REVIEWS side label from the reference.</li>\n<li>A comparison against your supplied screenshot, followed by correction of any differences.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Data and template checks.</strong> I will verify genuine collection-specific product reviews, the source integration, accurate averages/totals, verified-status metadata, absence of hardcoded example values, no conflicting review markup, and the no-review case. Check all assigned collection templates and a newly created collection using the default configuration.</p>\n<p><strong>Why this work is needed.</strong> This completes the exact feature requested in the document, including appearance, real review data and template inheritance. The reference values 4.7 and 195 are examples; the displayed values must be calculated from the actual data as your document also requires.</p>\n<p><strong>Schema dependency.</strong> The referenced hipuffy/nothingbutcanna implementations have not been inspected. I need the relevant reference code or access to compare the requested schema with those implementations. Google also limits review snippets to specific items rather than categories/lists. I will discuss this compatibility limitation with you before changing the requested schema treatment. Replicating the reference code does not guarantee search-star eligibility. <a href=\"https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/structured-data/review-snippet\">Google review guidance</a></p>\n<p><strong>How I will verify completion.</strong> The design matches the reference, controls work, real collection data drives the cards and summary, the required template behavior is verified, and the requested schema treatment is documented and validated. I will retain the existing section and correct it where necessary.</p>\n<p><strong>Estimate: 3 hours, including point 14.</strong> Assumes the current carousel and review connection are retained, the final reference is supplied, and remaining work is limited styling/data/template corrections. I need to agree the reference-schema treatment with you before starting.</p>\n<h2 id=\"issue-13\">13. Website performance recommendations</h2>\n<p><strong>Your requested change.</strong> Improve website performance with reference to the supplied mobile and desktop PageSpeed reports. The document specifies no target score and no separate theme-rebuild request.</p>\n<p><strong>What I found.</strong> The fresh report from 3 November 2026 shows the following lab measurements:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Metric</th>\n<th align=\"right\">Document mobile</th>\n<th align=\"right\">November mobile</th>\n<th align=\"right\">Document desktop</th>\n<th align=\"right\">November desktop</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Performance score</td>\n<td align=\"right\">43</td>\n<td align=\"right\">59</td>\n<td align=\"right\">52</td>\n<td align=\"right\">60</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>First Contentful Paint</td>\n<td align=\"right\">6.3 s</td>\n<td align=\"right\">1.8 s</td>\n<td align=\"right\">0.9 s</td>\n<td align=\"right\">0.5 s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Largest Contentful Paint</td>\n<td align=\"right\">22.5 s</td>\n<td align=\"right\">11.7 s</td>\n<td align=\"right\">2.3 s</td>\n<td align=\"right\">2.1 s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Total Blocking Time</td>\n<td align=\"right\">490 ms</td>\n<td align=\"right\">250 ms</td>\n<td align=\"right\">520 ms</td>\n<td align=\"right\">650 ms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cumulative Layout Shift</td>\n<td align=\"right\">0.012</td>\n<td align=\"right\">0.133</td>\n<td align=\"right\">0.12</td>\n<td align=\"right\">0.002</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>These runs used different tool versions and site content, so they are not a controlled attribution of improvement to particular changes. The current PageSpeed real-user assessment passes Core Web Vitals, while the simulated mobile load still shows a slow LCP.</p>\n<p><strong>What I will do.</strong> I will address measured performance diagnostics in the existing implementation: render-blocking resources, unused script/style loading, long main-thread tasks, image dimensions/layout shifts and current image delivery. The fresh mobile report estimates 1,120 ms render-blocking savings, 627 KiB unused JavaScript and 569 KiB image-delivery savings. These are investigation leads rather than guaranteed additive gains. I will coordinate this with point 5 so image work is counted once.</p>\n<p><strong>Why this work is needed.</strong> The recommendations respond to the performance item actually requested, using measured bottlenecks rather than introducing an unrelated redesign.</p>\n<p><strong>How I will verify completion.</strong> Record a baseline and repeat comparable mobile/desktop tests after changes; document metric improvements and remaining diagnostics; confirm affected storefront functions continue working. I will agree the measurable target with you before starting, since your document does not set one. My estimate does not assume a guaranteed 90+ score.</p>\n<p><strong>Estimate: 5 hours, excluding point 5.</strong> Covers targeted changes to measured bottlenecks in the existing implementation and repeat/functional checks. I need to agree the performance target with you first; this estimate does not cover a theme rebuild or guarantee an unspecified score.</p>\n<p>Fresh evidence: <a href=\"https://pagespeed.web.dev/analysis/https-www-tillmanstranquils-com/d8yumep20i?form_factor=mobile\">mobile PageSpeed report</a> and <a href=\"https://pagespeed.web.dev/analysis/https-www-tillmanstranquils-com/d8yumep20i?form_factor=desktop\">desktop PageSpeed report</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"issue-14\">14. Remove the old collection reviews block</h2>\n<p><strong>Your requested changes.</strong> Remove the specified large single-review banner from every collection and replace it with the new card-carousel design described in issue 12.</p>\n<p><strong>What I found.</strong> The old large banner was not found on the eight checked collections, while the new section is present. That supports observed replacement in the sample; it does not establish all template assignments. A separate small inline testimonial on CBD Mints has not been established as the old banner identified in the document.</p>\n<p><strong>What I will do.</strong> I will inspect all assigned collection templates, confirm that the specified old section is removed, and complete the replacement-design/data checklist under issue 12. Avoid assuming that changing only the default template covers collections with alternative templates.</p>\n<p><strong>Why this work is needed.</strong> This closes the specific replacement instruction across the requested scope without treating the same new carousel as a second build.</p>\n<p><strong>How I will verify completion.</strong> The specified old banner is absent from every applicable collection template, and its replacement satisfies issue 12&#39;s reference design and real-data requirements.</p>\n<p><strong>Estimate: 0 additional minutes.</strong> Old-block removal/coverage checks are included in point 12; the same carousel is not estimated twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"issue-15\">15. Category description in a left sidebar</h2>\n<p><strong>Your requested changes.</strong> Move the category description from its full-width position above products into a left column, keep products to its right, apply the change to all categories through the collection template, and stack the description above the products on mobile without breaking the grid.</p>\n<p><strong>What I found.</strong> CBD Mints already places the description inside a roughly 290-px left aside, with the product grid to its right, at the observed desktop viewport. I have not yet verified the layout at actual mobile widths or across every collection template; those checks are included in my remaining work.</p>\n<p><strong>What I will do.</strong> I will:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Inspect the shared description/grid implementation and every collection-template assignment.</li>\n<li>Retain the observed desktop positioning and correct any other category templates still using the old full-width arrangement.</li>\n<li>Verify actual phone-width rendering with the description above products as specified.</li>\n<li>Check grid widths, product-card wrapping and overflow with long and short descriptions.</li>\n<li>Confirm filters and sorting still work with the requested placement.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Why this work is needed.</strong> This completes the same two-column and mobile-stack arrangement requested in the document. An expandable or relocated mobile introduction is not assumed as a substitute.</p>\n<p><strong>How I will verify completion.</strong> All applicable categories inherit the requested layout; desktop description is left of the grid; mobile description is above products; no clipping or horizontal overflow occurs; collection controls still work.</p>\n<p><strong>Estimate: 1 hour 30 minutes.</strong> Covers shared-template coverage, actual phone-width checks and limited responsive fixes to the desktop implementation already observed.</p>\n<h2>What I will deliver</h2>\n<p>I will send a completion record organized by your original 15 points. It will show the affected URLs/templates, changes made, verification results and any point still awaiting an agreed input or supported implementation. I will include the relevant redirect checks, review/schema checks and before/after performance measurements.</p>\n<p>I will reconcile the 23 orphan-page candidates, the 98 image URLs representing 57 source assets, the affected-product export, the applicable collection aliases and the shared templates covered by your instructions. Points 4 and 14 will remain linked to points 1 and 12 so the same work is not counted twice.</p>\n<h2>References</h2>\n<p>I have used your correction document, <em>Error corrections - tillmanstranquils.com v2.docx</em>, dated 8 June 2026, as the scope for this proposal. The site findings are from my 3 November review of <a href=\"https://www.tillmanstranquils.com/\">Tillmans Tranquils</a>. The mobile and desktop PageSpeed results are linked under point 13.</p>\n<p>The implementation limitations noted under the relevant points are supported by <a href=\"https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/online-store/menus-and-links/url-redirect\">Shopify&#39;s redirect documentation</a>, <a href=\"https://developers.google.com/search/updates\">Google&#39;s FAQ-feature updates</a> and <a href=\"https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/structured-data/review-snippet\">Google&#39;s review guidance</a>. I will discuss the affected requirements with you before implementing a different method or changing the agreed scope.</p>\n<footer>Website review dated 3 November 2026. My estimates and proposed delivery date are subject to the access and decisions listed above.</footer>"
